00:01:05,204 --> 00:01:05,474
you.

33
00:01:05,654 --> 00:01:07,283
Second, the psychology:

34
00:01:07,283 --> 00:01:09,903
the design tricks that make you spend

35
00:01:09,903 --> 00:01:11,319
more and pay longer.

36
00:01:11,499 --> 00:01:13,039
And third, the playbook:

37
00:01:13,039 --> 00:01:14,772
how to flip the deal so the

38
00:01:14,772 --> 00:01:15,991
card works for you,

39
00:01:15,991 --> 00:01:17,595
not the other way around.

40
00:01:17,945 --> 00:01:18,609
By the end,

41
00:01:18,609 --> 00:01:20,661
you'll know whether your card is a

42
00:01:20,661 --> 00:01:21,567
tool or a trap.

43
00:01:21,747 --> 00:01:23,082
Let's start with the money.

44
00:01:23,432 --> 00:01:25,835
Every number in this video comes from

45
00:01:25,835 --> 00:01:26,810
a named source.

46
00:01:26,990 --> 00:01:29,217
Interest and fee totals come from the

47
00:01:29,217 --> 00:01:32,406
Consumer Financial Protection Bureau's reports on the

48
00:01:32,406 --> 00:01:33,550
credit card market.

49
00:01:33,730 --> 00:01:36,412
Interest rates come from the Federal Reserve.

50
00:01:36,592 --> 00:01:39,005
Research on card payments comes from the

51
00:01:39,005 --> 00:01:40,876
Federal Reserve Bank of Boston.

52
00:01:41,056 --> 00:01:42,807
Where I show an example,

53
00:01:42,807 --> 00:01:44,632
it's stated, simple math.

54
00:01:44,812 --> 00:01:45,934
Card terms vary,

55
00:01:45,934 --> 00:01:48,318
so always read your own agreement.

56
00:01:48,498 --> 00:01:49,821
This is education,

57
00:01:49,821 --> 00:01:51,365
not financial advice.

58
00:01:51,716 --> 00:01:52,979
Before the numbers,

59
00:01:52,979 --> 00:01:53,910
a quick story.

60
00:01:54,090 --> 00:01:56,541
Because to understand why cards work the

61
00:01:56,541 --> 00:01:57,277
way they do,

62
00:01:57,277 --> 00:01:59,361
it helps to know how they started.

63
00:01:59,711 --> 00:02:00,894
In nineteen fifty,

64
00:02:00,894 --> 00:02:03,457
a company called Diners Club launched a

65
00:02:03,457 --> 00:02:05,429
card that let members pay at a

66
00:02:05,429 --> 00:02:07,796
group of restaurants in New York and

67
00:02:07,796 --> 00:02:08,848
settle up later.

68
00:02:09,028 --> 00:02:10,200
It was a charge card.

69
00:02:10,380 --> 00:02:12,331
You paid the full bill each month.

70
00:02:12,511 --> 00:02:14,719
The business made money mainly from the

71
00:02:14,719 --> 00:02:16,871
restaurants, which paid a fee on every

72
00:02:16,871 --> 00:02:17,155
meal.

73
00:02:17,335 --> 00:02:19,367
That idea, that the merchant pays to

74
00:02:19,367 --> 00:02:20,270
accept the card,

75
00:02:20,270 --> 00:02:21,907
is still at the center of the

76
00:02:21,907 --> 00:02:22,640
system today.

77
00:02:22,991 --> 00:02:24,820
In the late nineteen fifties,

78
00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:27,217
Bank of America took the idea further.

79
00:02:27,397 --> 00:02:29,007
In Fresno, California,

80
00:02:29,007 --> 00:02:32,082
it mailed tens of thousands of unsolicited

81
00:02:32,082 --> 00:02:33,986
credit cards to residents,

82
00:02:33,986 --> 00:02:36,841
a famous experiment known as the Fresno

83
00:02:36,841 --> 00:02:37,207
drop.

84
00:02:37,387 --> 00:02:40,040
These cards let people carry a balance

85
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:41,297
and pay over time,

86
00:02:41,297 --> 00:02:42,275
with interest.

87
00:02:42,455 --> 00:02:45,469
Losses from fraud and unpaid balances were

88
00:02:45,469 --> 00:02:46,545
large at first,

89
00:02:46,545 --> 00:02:49,846
but the model eventually became BankAmericard,

90
00:02:49,846 --> 00:02:51,569
which later became Visa.

91
00:02:51,919 --> 00:02:53,451
Then came a turning point.

92
00:02:53,631 --> 00:02:55,228
In nineteen seventy eight,

93
00:02:55,228 --> 00:02:57,501
a Supreme Court decision known as the

94
00:02:57,501 --> 00:03:00,388
Marquette case allowed national banks to charge

95
00:03:00,388 --> 00:03:02,783
the interest rate allowed in their home

96
00:03:02,783 --> 00:03:04,626
state to customers nationwide.

97
00:03:04,806 --> 00:03:07,339
Some states soon loosened their interest rate

98
00:03:07,339 --> 00:03:09,252
limits to attract card businesses.

99
00:03:09,432 --> 00:03:11,130
That's a big part of why so

100
00:03:11,130 --> 00:03:13,582
many card companies are based in states

101
00:03:13,582 --> 00:03:15,531
like South Dakota and Delaware,

102
00:03:15,531 --> 00:03:17,291
and why card rates can be so

103
00:03:17,291 --> 00:03:17,606
high.

104
00:03:17,956 --> 00:03:20,069
Fast forward to two thousand nine.

105
00:03:20,249 --> 00:03:22,119
After the financial crisis,

106
00:03:22,119 --> 00:03:24,614
Congress passed the Credit CARD Act.

107
00:03:24,794 --> 00:03:27,742
It limited surprise rate increases on existing

108
00:03:27,742 --> 00:03:30,177
balances, required clearer statements,

109
00:03:30,177 --> 00:03:32,613
including the minimum payment warning,

110
00:03:32,613 --> 00:03:34,407
and restricted certain fees.

111
00:03:34,587 --> 00:03:35,818
It made cards fairer.

112
00:03:35,998 --> 00:03:37,878
But it didn't change the core business.

113
00:03:38,058 --> 00:03:40,457
Interest from people who carry balances,

114
00:03:40,457 --> 00:03:42,795
and fees from merchants on every swipe.

115
00:03:43,146 --> 00:03:43,935
Angle one.

116
00:03:44,115 --> 00:03:46,228
The eight main ways card companies make

117
00:03:46,228 --> 00:03:46,553
money.

118
00:03:46,733 --> 00:03:47,569
Some you know.

119
00:03:47,749 --> 00:03:49,200
A few you probably don't.

120
00:03:49,551 --> 00:03:50,572
Way number one.

121
00:03:50,752 --> 00:03:51,356
Interest.

122
00:03:51,536 --> 00:03:53,463
By far the biggest money maker.

123
00:03:53,814 --> 00:03:55,917
When you don't pay your full statement

124
00:03:55,917 --> 00:03:57,245
balance by the due date,

125
00:03:57,245 --> 00:03:59,514
the card charges interest on what's left.

126
00:03:59,694 --> 00:04:01,754
According to the Federal Reserve,

127
00:04:01,754 --> 00:04:04,126
the average rate on card accounts that

128
00:04:04,126 --> 00:04:06,810
were charged interest has been above twenty

129
00:04:06,810 --> 00:04:08,309
percent in recent years.

130
00:04:08,489 --> 00:04:11,148
That's higher than almost any other common

131
00:04:11,148 --> 00:04:12,540
type of consumer loan.

132
00:04:12,891 --> 00:04:14,168
Here's what that means.

133
00:04:14,348 --> 00:04:16,956
Carry a three thousand dollar balance all

134
00:04:16,956 --> 00:04:18,674
year at twenty two percent,

135
00:04:18,674 --> 00:04:21,028
and you pay roughly six hundred sixty

136
00:04:21,028 --> 00:04:22,300
dollars in interest.

137
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:24,802
That's six hundred sixty dollars that buys

138
00:04:24,802 --> 00:04:25,244
nothing.

139
00:04:25,424 --> 00:04:26,861
Inside the industry,

140
00:04:26,861 --> 00:04:29,449
people who carry a balance are often

141
00:04:29,449 --> 00:04:30,671
called revolvers.

142
00:04:30,851 --> 00:04:33,506
They're the most profitable customers a card

143
00:04:33,506 --> 00:04:34,230
company has.

144
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:36,785
There's a quiet detail most people miss.

145
00:04:36,965 --> 00:04:39,357
Card interest usually compounds daily.

146
00:04:39,537 --> 00:04:41,405
The card takes your yearly rate,

147
00:04:41,405 --> 00:04:43,681
divides it by three hundred sixty five,

148
00:04:43,681 --> 00:04:45,724
and applies it to your balance each

149
00:04:45,724 --> 00:04:45,957
day.

150
00:04:46,137 --> 00:04:48,174
Interest gets added to the balance,

151
00:04:48,174 --> 00:04:50,617
and tomorrow's interest is charged on that

152
00:04:50,617 --> 00:04:51,432
bigger number.

153
00:04:51,612 --> 00:04:54,448
It's the same compounding that grows investments,

154
00:04:54,448 --> 00:04:55,838
but working against you.

155
00:04:56,018 --> 00:04:57,954
Try this: find the APR on your

156
00:04:57,954 --> 00:04:59,245
statement right now.

157
00:04:59,425 --> 00:05:00,448
If you don't know it,

158
00:05:00,448 --> 00:05:01,666
that's the first problem.

159
00:05:02,016 --> 00:05:03,002
Way number two.

160
00:05:03,182 --> 00:05:06,654
Swipe fees, also called interchange fees.

161
00:05:07,003 --> 00:05:09,143
Every time you pay with a card,

162
00:05:09,143 --> 00:05:10,592
the store pays a fee,

163
00:05:10,592 --> 00:05:13,422
often somewhere around two percent of the

164
00:05:13,422 --> 00:05:16,942
purchase, sometimes more for premium rewards cards.

165
00:05:17,122 --> 00:05:19,151
Part goes to the bank that issued

166
00:05:19,151 --> 00:05:21,241
your card, part to the network and

167
00:05:21,241 --> 00:05:22,102
the processor.

168
00:05:22,282 --> 00:05:23,896
Across the whole country,

169
00:05:23,896 --> 00:05:26,672
merchants pay well over one hundred billion

170
00:05:26,672 --> 00:05:29,190
dollars a year in card processing fees.

171
00:05:29,540 --> 00:05:30,812
You might think,

172
00:05:30,812 --> 00:05:32,560
great, the store pays,

173
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:33,116
not me.

174
00:05:33,296 --> 00:05:35,747
But stores build those costs into their

175
00:05:35,747 --> 00:05:36,187
prices.

176
00:05:36,367 --> 00:05:38,747
That means everyone pays a little more,

177
00:05:38,747 --> 00:05:41,005
including people who pay with cash or

178
00:05:41,005 --> 00:05:41,371
debit.

179
00:05:41,551 --> 00:05:44,254
Researchers at the Federal Reserve Bank of

180
00:05:44,254 --> 00:05:46,506
Boston studied this and found that,

181
00:05:46,506 --> 00:05:49,144
on average, cash users end up subsidizing

182
00:05:49,144 --> 00:05:52,232
card users, especially those with rewards cards.

183
00:05:52,582 --> 00:05:54,220
This is how rewards are funded.

184
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:56,389
That two percent cash back isn't free

185
00:05:56,389 --> 00:05:57,465
money from the bank.

186
00:05:57,645 --> 00:06:00,063
It's largely recycled from swipe fees,

187
00:06:00,063 --> 00:06:02,799
which come from higher prices for everyone.

188
00:06:02,979 --> 00:06:04,082
If you pay in full,

189
00:06:04,082 --> 00:06:05,417
you can come out ahead.

190
00:06:05,597 --> 00:06:07,175
If you carry a balance,

191
00:06:07,175 --> 00:06:09,437
the interest you pay can wipe out

192
00:06:09,437 --> 00:06:11,426
every reward many times over.

193
00:06:11,606 --> 00:06:14,419
Try this: compare your rewards earned this

194
00:06:14,419 --> 00:06:16,563
year with the interest you paid.

195
00:06:16,743 --> 00:06:18,369
Be honest about which is bigger.

196
00:06:18,719 --> 00:06:19,938
Way number three.

197
00:06:20,118 --> 00:06:21,186
Late fees.

198
00:06:21,536 --> 00:06:22,562
Miss a due date,

199
00:06:22,562 --> 00:06:23,460
even by a day,

200
00:06:23,460 --> 00:06:25,512
and many cards charge a late fee

201
00:06:25,512 --> 00:06:27,821
that can be around thirty dollars or

202
00:06:27,821 --> 00:06:28,142
more.

203
00:06:28,322 --> 00:06:31,874
The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau estimated that

204
00:06:31,874 --> 00:06:35,427
card companies charged roughly fourteen billion dollars

205
00:06:35,427 --> 00:06:37,559
in late fees in twenty twenty two

206
00:06:37,559 --> 00:06:37,946
alone.

207
00:06:38,126 --> 00:06:40,007
That's a lot of forgotten due dates.

208
00:06:40,357 --> 00:06:42,400
Late fees often don't come alone.

209
00:06:42,580 --> 00:06:44,859
A late payment can trigger a higher

210
00:06:44,859 --> 00:06:46,292
penalty interest rate,

211
00:06:46,292 --> 00:06:47,985
cancel a promotional rate,

212
00:06:47,985 --> 00:06:50,069
and, if it's thirty days late or

213
00:06:50,069 --> 00:06:52,414
more, show up on your credit report.

214
00:06:52,594 --> 00:06:55,084
One forgotten payment can cost the fee,

215
00:06:55,084 --> 00:06:57,446
months of higher interest and a lower

216
00:06:57,446 --> 00:06:57,830
score.

217
00:06:58,180 --> 00:07:00,137
Card companies don't need you to be

218
00:07:00,137 --> 00:07:00,919
irresponsible.

219
00:07:01,099 --> 00:07:02,771
They just need you to be human.

220
00:07:02,951 --> 00:07:05,049
Busy weeks, a changed due date,

221
00:07:05,049 --> 00:07:07,282
a payment that didn't go through.

222
00:07:07,462 --> 00:07:09,703
The easiest defense is automation.

223
00:07:09,883 --> 00:07:12,270
Try this: set autopay for at least

224
00:07:12,270 --> 00:07:14,096
the minimum on every card,

225
00:07:14,096 --> 00:07:15,571
so you're never late,

226
00:07:15,571 --> 00:07:17,889
then pay the rest manually or set

227
00:07:17,889 --> 00:07:19,856
autopay to the full balance.

228
00:07:20,205 --> 00:07:21,239
Way number four.

229
00:07:21,419 --> 00:07:22,417
Annual fees.

230
00:07:22,767 --> 00:07:25,415
Many basic cards have no annual fee.

231
00:07:25,595 --> 00:07:27,929
But premium travel and rewards cards can

232
00:07:27,929 --> 00:07:30,264
charge from under one hundred dollars to

233
00:07:30,264 --> 00:07:32,073
several hundred dollars a year.

234
00:07:32,253 --> 00:07:34,948
In exchange, they offer bigger rewards,

235
00:07:34,948 --> 00:07:36,676
travel credits and perks.

236
00:07:36,856 --> 00:07:38,389
For the card company,

237
00:07:38,389 --> 00:07:40,213
the annual fee is steady,

238
00:07:40,213 --> 00:07:43,423
predictable income, paid whether you use the

239
00:07:43,423 --> 00:07:44,298
card or not.

240
00:07:44,648 --> 00:07:45,855
The perks are real,

241
00:07:45,855 --> 00:07:48,015
but only if you actually use them.

242
00:07:48,195 --> 00:07:49,982
A five hundred dollar card with a

243
00:07:49,982 --> 00:07:52,256
three hundred dollar travel credit is only

244
00:07:52,256 --> 00:07:54,042
worth it if you'd have spent that

245
00:07:54,042 --> 00:07:55,720
three hundred on travel anyway,

246
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:57,453
and the rest of the perks matter

247
00:07:57,453 --> 00:07:57,832
to you.

248
00:07:58,012 --> 00:08:00,353
Many people pay for benefits they forget

249
00:08:00,353 --> 00:08:00,879
to claim.

250
00:08:01,229 --> 00:08:04,562
Card companies count on something called breakage,

251
00:08:04,562 --> 00:08:07,227
benefits that people are entitled to but

252
00:08:07,227 --> 00:08:07,894
never use.

253
00:08:08,074 --> 00:08:10,430
Unused credits, expired points,

254
00:08:10,430 --> 00:08:12,938
lounge passes that sit in an app.

255
00:08:13,118 --> 00:08:14,848
Every unused perk is profit.

256
00:08:15,028 --> 00:08:17,489
Try this: list every benefit of your

257
00:08:17,489 --> 00:08:19,882
fee card and what you actually used

258
00:08:19,882 --> 00:08:20,566
last year.

259
00:08:20,746 --> 00:08:22,769
If the total is less than the

260
00:08:22,769 --> 00:08:24,862
fee, ask for a no fee version,

261
00:08:24,862 --> 00:08:26,328
or a retention offer,

262
00:08:26,328 --> 00:08:28,072
before your renewal date.

263
00:08:28,421 --> 00:08:29,513
Way number five.

264
00:08:29,693 --> 00:08:31,330
Balance transfer fees.

265
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:34,373
Balance transfer offers sound like a gift.

266
00:08:34,553 --> 00:08:36,339
Move your debt to a new card

267
00:08:36,339 --> 00:08:38,571
at zero percent interest for a year

268
00:08:38,571 --> 00:08:39,081
or more.

269
00:08:39,261 --> 00:08:40,578
But most charge a fee,

270
00:08:40,578 --> 00:08:42,974
commonly around three to five percent of

271
00:08:42,974 --> 00:08:44,172
the amount you move.

272
00:08:44,352 --> 00:08:46,506
Move ten thousand dollars at a three

273
00:08:46,506 --> 00:08:48,780
percent fee, and you pay three hundred

274
00:08:48,780 --> 00:08:49,797
dollars up front.

275
00:08:50,146 --> 00:08:51,998
That can still be a great deal

276
00:08:51,998 --> 00:08:55,023
compared with paying twenty two percent interest.

277
00:08:55,203 --> 00:08:57,165
The trap is what happens next.

278
00:08:57,345 --> 00:08:59,448
If the balance isn't paid off when

279
00:08:59,448 --> 00:09:00,624
the promotion ends,

280
00:09:00,624 --> 00:09:02,233
the regular rate kicks in.

281
00:09:02,413 --> 00:09:03,547
And many people,

282
00:09:03,547 --> 00:09:05,605
relieved by the zero percent,

283
00:09:05,605 --> 00:09:07,591
keep using the old card too.

284
00:09:07,771 --> 00:09:09,779
Now they have two balances.

285
00:09:10,129 --> 00:09:12,270
Card companies know a share of people

286
00:09:12,270 --> 00:09:13,485
won't finish in time.

287
00:09:13,665 --> 00:09:15,929
That's part of why the offers exist.

288
00:09:16,109 --> 00:09:17,238
Used with a plan,

289
00:09:17,238 --> 00:09:19,430
a balance transfer can save you a

290
00:09:19,430 --> 00:09:19,696
lot.

291
00:09:19,876 --> 00:09:20,884
Used as relief,

292
00:09:20,884 --> 00:09:22,697
it just delays the problem.

293
00:09:22,877 --> 00:09:24,984
Try this: before transferring,

294
00:09:24,984 --> 00:09:27,441
divide the balance by the number of

295
00:09:27,441 --> 00:09:28,775
promotional months.

296
00:09:28,955 --> 00:09:30,887
If you can't pay that amount every

297
00:09:30,887 --> 00:09:33,273
month, the offer probably won't solve your

298
00:09:33,273 --> 00:09:33,727
problem.

299
00:09:34,076 --> 00:09:35,307
Way number six.

300
00:09:35,487 --> 00:09:36,613
Cash advances.

301
00:09:36,963 --> 00:09:39,255
Using your credit card to get cash

302
00:09:39,255 --> 00:09:41,142
from an ATM is called a cash

303
00:09:41,142 --> 00:09:43,568
advance, and it's usually one of the

304
00:09:43,568 --> 00:09:45,590
most expensive ways to borrow.

305
00:09:45,770 --> 00:09:47,858
There's often an upfront fee,

306
00:09:47,858 --> 00:09:50,810
commonly around five percent or a minimum

307
00:09:50,810 --> 00:09:51,818
dollar amount.

308
00:09:51,998 --> 00:09:54,050
The interest rate is often higher than

309
00:09:54,050 --> 00:09:55,075
your purchase rate.

310
00:09:55,255 --> 00:09:57,496
And there's usually no grace period,

311
00:09:57,496 --> 00:09:59,675
so interest starts the day you take

312
00:09:59,675 --> 00:10:00,236
the cash.

313
00:10:00,585 --> 00:10:02,725
Take five hundred dollars in a cash

314
00:10:02,725 --> 00:10:04,864
advance, and you might pay a twenty

315
00:10:04,864 --> 00:10:06,514
five dollar fee right away,

316
00:10:06,514 --> 00:10:08,653
plus interest at a higher rate from

317
00:10:08,653 --> 00:10:09,142
day one.

318
00:10:09,322 --> 00:10:12,014
Some things also count as cash advances

319
00:10:12,014 --> 00:10:13,739
without looking like one,

320
00:10:13,739 --> 00:10:15,534
like some money transfers,

321
00:10:15,534 --> 00:10:17,605
buying certain gambling chips,

322
00:10:17,605 --> 00:10:20,297
or some purchases that are treated like

323
00:10:20,297 --> 00:10:20,642
cash.

324
00:10:20,822 --> 00:10:22,180
Check your card's terms.

325
00:10:22,530 --> 00:10:25,381
People usually take cash advances when they're

326
00:10:25,381 --> 00:10:27,921
already stressed, which is why they're so

327
00:10:27,921 --> 00:10:28,602
profitable.

328
00:10:28,782 --> 00:10:30,726
The real fix is upstream:

329
00:10:30,726 --> 00:10:33,447
a small emergency fund so you never

330
00:10:33,447 --> 00:10:34,146
need one.

331
00:10:34,326 --> 00:10:36,457
Try this: ask your bank to lower

332
00:10:36,457 --> 00:10:38,720
or turn off the cash advance limit

333
00:10:38,720 --> 00:10:39,586
on your card.

334
00:10:39,766 --> 00:10:41,891
It removes the option on your worst

335
00:10:41,891 --> 00:10:42,134
day.

336
00:10:42,483 --> 00:10:43,470
Way number seven.

337
00:10:43,650 --> 00:10:45,357
Foreign transaction fees.

338
00:10:45,706 --> 00:10:47,449
Many cards charge a fee,

339
00:10:47,449 --> 00:10:49,408
often around three percent,

340
00:10:49,408 --> 00:10:52,312
on purchases made in another currency or

341
00:10:52,312 --> 00:10:54,344
processed by a foreign bank.

342
00:10:54,524 --> 00:10:57,313
That includes buying something online from an

343
00:10:57,313 --> 00:10:59,729
overseas store, even if you never leave

344
00:10:59,729 --> 00:11:00,039
home.

345
00:11:00,219 --> 00:11:02,569
Spend two thousand dollars on a trip

346
00:11:02,569 --> 00:11:05,245
abroad, and that's about sixty dollars in

347
00:11:05,245 --> 00:11:05,571
fees.

348
00:11:05,921 --> 00:11:08,314
There's a related trap at the register

349
00:11:08,314 --> 00:11:08,754
abroad.

350
00:11:08,934 --> 00:11:11,361
Sometimes the card machine asks if you

351
00:11:11,361 --> 00:11:13,468
want to pay in dollars instead of

352
00:11:13,468 --> 00:11:14,681
the local currency.

353
00:11:14,861 --> 00:11:16,312
It sounds convenient.

354
00:11:16,492 --> 00:11:17,784
But that conversion,

355
00:11:17,784 --> 00:11:20,045
called dynamic currency conversion,

356
00:11:20,045 --> 00:11:22,370
often uses a worse exchange rate set

357
00:11:22,370 --> 00:11:24,178
by the merchant's processor.

358
00:11:24,528 --> 00:11:27,217
Many cards, especially travel cards,

359
00:11:27,217 --> 00:11:30,577
don't charge foreign transaction fees at all.

360
00:11:30,757 --> 00:11:32,394
So this one is easy to avoid

361
00:11:32,394 --> 00:11:33,973
if you check before you go.

362
00:11:34,153 --> 00:11:36,271
Try this: before your next trip or

363
00:11:36,271 --> 00:11:37,953
international online order,

364
00:11:37,953 --> 00:11:39,947
look up your card's foreign fee.

365
00:11:40,127 --> 00:11:41,491
And at foreign terminals,

366
00:11:41,491 --> 00:11:43,455
generally choose to pay in the local

367
00:11:43,455 --> 00:11:43,946
currency.

368
00:11:44,296 --> 00:11:45,214
Way number eight.

369
00:11:45,394 --> 00:11:46,462
Penalty APRs.

370
00:11:46,811 --> 00:11:48,814
Many cards have a penalty rate,

371
00:11:48,814 --> 00:11:51,139
a much higher interest rate that can

372
00:11:51,139 --> 00:11:52,754
apply after you pay late,

373
00:11:52,754 --> 00:11:54,951
often if you're sixty days or more

374
00:11:54,951 --> 00:11:55,403
behind.

375
00:11:55,583 --> 00:11:58,216
Penalty rates on some cards approach thirty

376
00:11:58,216 --> 00:11:58,706
percent.

377
00:11:58,886 --> 00:12:01,381
Federal rules under the Credit CARD Act

378
00:12:01,381 --> 00:12:03,620
of two thousand nine limit when and

379
00:12:03,620 --> 00:12:05,220
how these can be applied,

380
00:12:05,220 --> 00:12:07,459
and require the issuer to review it

381
00:12:07,459 --> 00:12:09,442
later, but it can still be very

382
00:12:09,442 --> 00:12:11,042
expensive while it lasts.

383
00:12:11,391 --> 00:12:13,925
Picture a five thousand dollar balance moving

384
00:12:13,925 --> 00:12:16,177
from twenty two percent to nearly thirty

385
00:12:16,177 --> 00:12:16,628
percent.

386
00:12:16,808 --> 00:12:19,236
That's roughly four hundred dollars more in

387
00:12:19,236 --> 00:12:20,252
interest per year,

388
00:12:20,252 --> 00:12:21,382
on top of late fees.

389
00:12:21,562 --> 00:12:23,748
It's the card company's way of charging

390
00:12:23,748 --> 00:12:26,159
more to customers it now considers riskier.

391
00:12:26,339 --> 00:12:28,694
And riskier customers who keep paying are

392
00:12:28,694 --> 00:12:29,613
very profitable.

393
00:12:29,963 --> 00:12:31,009
The good news:

394
00:12:31,009 --> 00:12:33,772
penalty rates are entirely avoidable.

395
00:12:33,952 --> 00:12:35,655
Pay at least the minimum,

396
00:12:35,655 --> 00:12:37,086
on time, every month.

397
00:12:37,266 --> 00:12:38,521
If you're struggling,

398
00:12:38,521 --> 00:12:40,492
call the issuer before you miss a

399
00:12:40,492 --> 00:12:40,970
payment.

400
00:12:41,150 --> 00:12:43,497
Many have hardship programs that can lower

401
00:12:43,497 --> 00:12:45,341
your rate or payment temporarily.

402
00:12:45,521 --> 00:12:48,117
Try this: save your card issuer's phone

403
00:12:48,117 --> 00:12:49,715
number in your contacts,

404
00:12:49,715 --> 00:12:51,512
under call before I'm late.

405
00:12:51,861 --> 00:12:53,301
So that's the money side.

406
00:12:53,481 --> 00:12:55,229
Interest, swipe fees,

407
00:12:55,229 --> 00:12:57,144
late fees, annual fees,

408
00:12:57,144 --> 00:12:58,975
balance transfer fees,

409
00:12:58,975 --> 00:13:02,804
cash advances, foreign fees and penalty rates.

410
00:13:02,984 --> 00:13:04,040
Notice something.

411
00:13:04,220 --> 00:13:06,426
Almost every one of them is avoidable.

412
00:13:06,606 --> 00:13:08,390
People who pay in full,

413
00:13:08,390 --> 00:13:09,708
on time, at home,

414
00:13:09,708 --> 00:13:11,336
pay close to nothing,

415
00:13:11,336 --> 00:13:13,352
and still collect rewards.

416
00:13:13,532 --> 00:13:15,569
People who carry a balance and slip

417
00:13:15,569 --> 00:13:17,315
up now and then fund the whole

418
00:13:17,315 --> 00:13:17,723
system.

419
00:13:18,072 --> 00:13:20,429
Here's the contrast in a single picture.

420
00:13:20,609 --> 00:13:22,955
A cardholder who pays in full can

421
00:13:22,955 --> 00:13:25,300
earn rewards and pay no interest.

422
00:13:25,480 --> 00:13:28,261
A cardholder who carries three thousand dollars

423
00:13:28,261 --> 00:13:30,509
at twenty two percent pays roughly six

424
00:13:30,509 --> 00:13:32,225
hundred sixty dollars a year.

425
00:13:32,575 --> 00:13:34,749
Now the part the card companies really

426
00:13:34,749 --> 00:13:35,664
don't advertise.

427
00:13:35,844 --> 00:13:38,025
How the product is designed to change

428
00:13:38,025 --> 00:13:38,851
your behavior.

429
00:13:39,200 --> 00:13:40,211
Angle two.

430
00:13:40,391 --> 00:13:41,528
The psychology.

431
00:13:41,708 --> 00:13:44,423
Five design tricks that keep you spending

432
00:13:44,423 --> 00:13:45,946
more and paying longer.

433
00:13:46,295 --> 00:13:47,317
Way number nine.

434
00:13:47,497 --> 00:13:49,018
The minimum payment anchor.

435
00:13:49,368 --> 00:13:51,642
Your statement shows several numbers:

436
00:13:51,642 --> 00:13:52,686
the full balance,

437
00:13:52,686 --> 00:13:54,837
and a much smaller minimum payment.

438
00:13:55,017 --> 00:13:58,114
Research by psychologist Neil Stewart found that

439
00:13:58,114 --> 00:14:00,566
showing the minimum payment can act as

440
00:14:00,566 --> 00:14:03,018
an anchor, a number your brain latches

441
00:14:03,018 --> 00:14:03,341
onto.

442
00:14:03,521 --> 00:14:04,711
In his experiments,

443
00:14:04,711 --> 00:14:07,153
people who saw a minimum payment tended

444
00:14:07,153 --> 00:14:09,344
to choose to repay less than people

445
00:14:09,344 --> 00:14:10,534
who didn't see one.

446
00:14:10,883 --> 00:14:13,534
The minimum is often calculated as a

447
00:14:13,534 --> 00:14:16,700
small percent of the balance plus interest.

448
00:14:16,880 --> 00:14:18,819
It's designed to keep your account in

449
00:14:18,819 --> 00:14:20,548
good standing, not to get you out

450
00:14:20,548 --> 00:14:20,967
of debt.

451
00:14:21,147 --> 00:14:22,040
At the minimum,

452
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:24,124
a balance of a few thousand dollars

453
00:14:24,124 --> 00:14:25,851
can take many years to clear,

454
00:14:25,851 --> 00:14:28,114
and the total interest can approach or

455
00:14:28,114 --> 00:14:30,377
even exceed what you originally spent.

456
00:14:30,726 --> 00:14:32,273
Since the Credit CARD Act,

457
00:14:32,273 --> 00:14:34,534
statements must show how long it would

458
00:14:34,534 --> 00:14:36,616
take to pay off your balance making

459
00:14:36,616 --> 00:14:37,925
only minimum payments,

460
00:14:37,925 --> 00:14:39,829
and how much you'd pay in total.

461
00:14:40,009 --> 00:14:42,017
Most people skip that box.

462
00:14:42,197 --> 00:14:44,121
Try this: find it on your next

463
00:14:44,121 --> 00:14:46,110
statement and read it out loud.

464
00:14:46,290 --> 00:14:48,707
Then choose a fixed monthly payment that

465
00:14:48,707 --> 00:14:50,762
clears the balance in the time you

466
00:14:50,762 --> 00:14:52,757
want, and set it as your autopay.

467
00:14:53,106 --> 00:14:54,175
Way number ten.

468
00:14:54,355 --> 00:14:56,398
Rewards that change how you spend.

469
00:14:56,747 --> 00:14:59,379
Points, miles and cash back feel like

470
00:14:59,379 --> 00:15:00,161
free money.

471
00:15:00,341 --> 00:15:02,233
And that feeling changes behavior.

472
00:15:02,413 --> 00:15:04,975
People may choose a card purchase over

473
00:15:04,975 --> 00:15:06,323
cash to earn points,

474
00:15:06,323 --> 00:15:08,009
spend a bit more to hit a

475
00:15:08,009 --> 00:15:10,301
bonus, or book a hotel they didn't

476
00:15:10,301 --> 00:15:12,189
need to keep a status level.

477
00:15:12,369 --> 00:15:13,971
The reward is small.

478
00:15:14,151 --> 00:15:15,997
The extra spending can be large.

479
00:15:16,346 --> 00:15:19,086
Sign up bonuses are the clearest example.

480
00:15:19,266 --> 00:15:22,085
Spend four thousand dollars in three months,

481
00:15:22,085 --> 00:15:23,109
get a big bonus.

482
00:15:23,289 --> 00:15:25,041
If you were going to spend that

483
00:15:25,041 --> 00:15:25,832
anyway, great.

484
00:15:26,012 --> 00:15:28,005
If the bonus makes you buy things

485
00:15:28,005 --> 00:15:29,213
to reach the target,

486
00:15:29,213 --> 00:15:31,447
the card company just turned a reward

487
00:15:31,447 --> 00:15:32,534
into new spending,

488
00:15:32,534 --> 00:15:34,104
and possibly new interest.

489
00:15:34,454 --> 00:15:36,788
Rewards also use gamification.

490
00:15:36,968 --> 00:15:39,244
Progress bars, status tiers,

491
00:15:39,244 --> 00:15:40,869
limited time offers,

492
00:15:40,869 --> 00:15:43,551
all designed to keep you engaged.

493
00:15:43,731 --> 00:15:46,228
Try this: treat rewards as a discount

494
00:15:46,228 --> 00:15:48,387
on spending you already planned,

495
00:15:48,387 --> 00:15:50,209
never as a reason to spend.

496
00:15:50,389 --> 00:15:52,542
And if you ever carry a balance,

497
00:15:52,542 --> 00:15:55,368
forget rewards completely and focus on the

498
00:15:55,368 --> 00:15:56,310
interest rate.

499
00:15:56,660 --> 00:15:57,682
Way number eleven.

500
00:15:57,862 --> 00:15:59,697
Deferred interest promotions.

501
00:16:00,046 --> 00:16:02,602
You've seen it at furniture and electronics

502
00:16:02,602 --> 00:16:03,019
stores.

503
00:16:03,199 --> 00:16:05,185
No interest if paid in full in

504
00:16:05,185 --> 00:16:06,113
twelve months.

505
00:16:06,293 --> 00:16:07,407
That wording matters.

506
00:16:07,587 --> 00:16:09,946
With many of these store card offers,

507
00:16:09,946 --> 00:16:12,178
if you don't pay the entire balance

508
00:16:12,178 --> 00:16:13,198
by the deadline,

509
00:16:13,198 --> 00:16:15,748
interest is charged back to the original

510
00:16:15,748 --> 00:16:17,916
purchase date, on the full amount,

511
00:16:17,916 --> 00:16:19,255
not just what's left.

512
00:16:19,605 --> 00:16:21,567
Say you buy a two thousand dollar

513
00:16:21,567 --> 00:16:24,005
couch on a twelve month deferred interest

514
00:16:24,005 --> 00:16:25,610
plan at twenty six percent,

515
00:16:25,610 --> 00:16:27,393
and you've paid it down to two

516
00:16:27,393 --> 00:16:29,474
hundred dollars when the year ends.

517
00:16:29,654 --> 00:16:31,125
On many of these plans,

518
00:16:31,125 --> 00:16:33,365
you could be charged roughly a year

519
00:16:33,365 --> 00:16:35,732
of interest on the full two thousand,

520
00:16:35,732 --> 00:16:37,779
potentially hundreds of dollars,

521
00:16:37,779 --> 00:16:38,547
all at once.

522
00:16:38,727 --> 00:16:42,287
The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau has warned

523
00:16:42,287 --> 00:16:44,312
consumers about exactly this.

524
00:16:44,661 --> 00:16:46,697
This is different from a true zero

525
00:16:46,697 --> 00:16:47,714
percent APR card,

526
00:16:47,714 --> 00:16:50,288
where interest only starts on the remaining

527
00:16:50,288 --> 00:16:51,964
balance after the promotion.

528
00:16:52,144 --> 00:16:54,062
The words if paid in full are

529
00:16:54,062 --> 00:16:55,186
the warning sign.

530
00:16:55,366 --> 00:16:57,404
Try this: if you use a deferred

531
00:16:57,404 --> 00:16:59,967
interest offer, divide the price by the

532
00:16:59,967 --> 00:17:01,151
months, minus one,

533
00:17:01,151 --> 00:17:03,386
and pay that amount every month so

534
00:17:03,386 --> 00:17:04,503
you finish early.

535
00:17:04,852 --> 00:17:05,886
Way number twelve.

536
00:17:06,066 --> 00:17:08,121
Automatic credit limit increases.

537
00:17:08,470 --> 00:17:11,029
Card companies often raise your limit over

538
00:17:11,029 --> 00:17:13,161
time, sometimes without you asking.

539
00:17:13,341 --> 00:17:14,827
It feels like a compliment.

540
00:17:15,007 --> 00:17:16,458
You've been trusted with more.

541
00:17:16,638 --> 00:17:18,562
And a higher limit can actually help

542
00:17:18,562 --> 00:17:20,433
your score, by lowering how much of

543
00:17:20,433 --> 00:17:22,304
your available credit you're using.

544
00:17:22,653 --> 00:17:23,995
But for some people,

545
00:17:23,995 --> 00:17:26,612
a higher limit simply becomes more room

546
00:17:26,612 --> 00:17:27,216
to spend.

547
00:17:27,396 --> 00:17:30,241
Research on consumer behavior has suggested that

548
00:17:30,241 --> 00:17:32,789
people often treat available credit as part

549
00:17:32,789 --> 00:17:34,211
of what they can afford.

550
00:17:34,391 --> 00:17:36,803
If you were already carrying a balance,

551
00:17:36,803 --> 00:17:38,782
a higher limit can mean a higher

552
00:17:38,782 --> 00:17:40,452
balance, and more interest.

553
00:17:40,801 --> 00:17:42,319
The key is knowing which type of

554
00:17:42,319 --> 00:17:43,031
person you are.

555
00:17:43,211 --> 00:17:45,321
If you pay in full every month,

556
00:17:45,321 --> 00:17:47,704
a higher limit is mostly a benefit.

557
00:17:47,884 --> 00:17:49,158
If you carry a balance,

558
00:17:49,158 --> 00:17:49,822
it's a risk.

559
00:17:50,002 --> 00:17:52,464
Try this: if limit increases tempt you,

560
00:17:52,464 --> 00:17:54,421
call and ask to keep your limit

561
00:17:54,421 --> 00:17:55,178
where it is,

562
00:17:55,178 --> 00:17:57,387
or set your own spending alert well

563
00:17:57,387 --> 00:17:57,955
below it.

564
00:17:58,305 --> 00:17:59,664
Way number thirteen.

565
00:17:59,844 --> 00:18:01,283
Making payment painless.

566
00:18:01,633 --> 00:18:03,510
Paying with cash makes you feel the

567
00:18:03,510 --> 00:18:04,153
money leave.

568
00:18:04,333 --> 00:18:06,655
Researchers call that the pain of paying.

569
00:18:06,835 --> 00:18:08,367
Cards reduce that pain,

570
00:18:08,367 --> 00:18:11,230
and studies have suggested people may spend

571
00:18:11,230 --> 00:18:12,895
more when paying by card.

572
00:18:13,075 --> 00:18:13,843
Tap to pay,

573
00:18:13,843 --> 00:18:16,915
phone wallets and one click checkouts reduce

574
00:18:16,915 --> 00:18:18,033
it even further.

575
00:18:18,213 --> 00:18:20,140
Less pain, more spending.

576
00:18:20,489 --> 00:18:23,229
Card companies and retailers work hard to

577
00:18:23,229 --> 00:18:24,298
remove friction.

578
00:18:24,478 --> 00:18:26,231
Saved cards in every app.

579
00:18:26,411 --> 00:18:27,781
Approval in a second.

580
00:18:27,961 --> 00:18:28,959
No signature.

581
00:18:29,139 --> 00:18:30,872
Each step makes buying easier,

582
00:18:30,872 --> 00:18:32,663
which is great for convenience,

583
00:18:32,663 --> 00:18:33,992
and great for spending.

584
00:18:34,172 --> 00:18:35,936
Convenience is the product,

585
00:18:35,936 --> 00:18:38,027
and your spending is the result.

586
00:18:38,376 --> 00:18:40,350
You don't have to give up convenience.

587
00:18:40,530 --> 00:18:42,889
You just need to add one deliberate

588
00:18:42,889 --> 00:18:43,293
pause.

589
00:18:43,473 --> 00:18:46,381
Try this: remove saved cards from shopping

590
00:18:46,381 --> 00:18:48,735
sites and apps you use for impulse

591
00:18:48,735 --> 00:18:49,081
buys.

592
00:18:49,261 --> 00:18:51,676
Keep them only for bills and essentials.

593
00:18:51,856 --> 00:18:53,919
That small pause is often enough to

594
00:18:53,919 --> 00:18:55,629
ask, do I actually want this?

595
00:18:55,979 --> 00:18:57,349
Here's something worth knowing.

596
00:18:57,529 --> 00:18:59,672
Card companies think about you as a

597
00:18:59,672 --> 00:19:01,877
journey, from the first offer to the

598
00:19:01,877 --> 00:19:02,673
last payment.

599
00:19:02,853 --> 00:19:04,211
Let me walk you through it.

600
00:19:04,560 --> 00:19:06,023
It starts with acquisition.

601
00:19:06,203 --> 00:19:08,108
Mail offers, app pop ups,

602
00:19:08,108 --> 00:19:10,545
and sign up bonuses at checkout.

603
00:19:10,725 --> 00:19:13,211
Card companies spend heavily to win new

604
00:19:13,211 --> 00:19:15,825
customers, because a good customer can be

605
00:19:15,825 --> 00:19:17,482
profitable for many years.

606
00:19:17,662 --> 00:19:20,010
That's why so many offers arrive in

607
00:19:20,010 --> 00:19:22,290
your mailbox, and why the bonus is

608
00:19:22,290 --> 00:19:22,759
so big.

609
00:19:22,939 --> 00:19:24,472
They expect to earn it back.

610
00:19:24,821 --> 00:19:27,167
Next comes activation and habit.

611
00:19:27,347 --> 00:19:28,798
The goal is to make the card

612
00:19:28,798 --> 00:19:29,471
your default.

613
00:19:29,651 --> 00:19:30,998
Put it in your phone wallet.

614
00:19:31,178 --> 00:19:33,163
Save it in your favorite apps.

615
00:19:33,343 --> 00:19:35,725
Earn extra points in your most common

616
00:19:35,725 --> 00:19:36,304
category.

617
00:19:36,484 --> 00:19:38,067
Once a card is the one you

618
00:19:38,067 --> 00:19:39,711
reach for without thinking,

619
00:19:39,711 --> 00:19:41,720
it tends to stay there for years.

620
00:19:42,069 --> 00:19:43,683
Then the profitable stage.

621
00:19:43,863 --> 00:19:45,332
If you carry a balance,

622
00:19:45,332 --> 00:19:47,056
interest flows every month.

623
00:19:47,236 --> 00:19:48,592
If you pay in full,

624
00:19:48,592 --> 00:19:50,162
swipe fees still flow.

625
00:19:50,342 --> 00:19:53,671
Limit increases, balance transfer offers and new

626
00:19:53,671 --> 00:19:55,613
promotions keep you engaged.

627
00:19:55,793 --> 00:19:57,534
And finally, retention.

628
00:19:57,714 --> 00:19:59,272
If you call to cancel,

629
00:19:59,272 --> 00:20:01,893
many issuers will offer bonus points,

630
00:20:01,893 --> 00:20:03,876
a waived fee or a lower rate

631
00:20:03,876 --> 00:20:04,726
to keep you.

632
00:20:04,906 --> 00:20:06,834
Which tells you something useful.

633
00:20:07,014 --> 00:20:08,794
Your business is worth more to them

634
00:20:08,794 --> 00:20:09,556
than you think.

635
00:20:09,906 --> 00:20:11,694
Knowing this journey gives you leverage.

636
00:20:11,874 --> 00:20:13,502
You can take the sign up bonus

637
00:20:13,502 --> 00:20:15,021
on spending you'd do anyway.

638
00:20:15,201 --> 00:20:17,282
You can choose which card becomes your

639
00:20:17,282 --> 00:20:17,720
default.

640
00:20:17,900 --> 00:20:20,035
You can call before your annual fee

641
00:20:20,035 --> 00:20:22,231
posts and ask for a retention offer.

642
00:20:22,411 --> 00:20:23,841
And you can walk away if the

643
00:20:23,841 --> 00:20:25,220
deal stops working for you.

644
00:20:25,400 --> 00:20:26,364
You're the customer.

645
00:20:26,544 --> 00:20:27,426
Act like one.

646
00:20:27,776 --> 00:20:28,833
Quick pause.

647
00:20:29,013 --> 00:20:30,866
If you just realized your card has

648
00:20:30,866 --> 00:20:32,283
been making money off you,

649
00:20:32,283 --> 00:20:33,645
subscribe to the channel.

650
00:20:33,825 --> 00:20:35,764
It's free, and it helps me make

651
00:20:35,764 --> 00:20:37,390
more videos like this one.

652
00:20:37,570 --> 00:20:39,416
Now, let's flip the deal.

653
00:20:39,765 --> 00:20:40,683
Angle three.

654
00:20:40,863 --> 00:20:41,594
The playbook.

655
00:20:41,774 --> 00:20:43,958
Ten rules that make your card company

656
00:20:43,958 --> 00:20:45,671
earn almost nothing from you,

657
00:20:45,671 --> 00:20:47,324
while you keep the benefits.

658
00:20:47,673 --> 00:20:48,405
Rule one.

659
00:20:48,585 --> 00:20:51,209
Pay the full statement balance every month.

660
00:20:51,389 --> 00:20:54,041
This single habit removes the biggest source

661
00:20:54,041 --> 00:20:54,945
of card profit,

662
00:20:54,945 --> 00:20:55,487
interest.

663
00:20:55,667 --> 00:20:57,825
Most cards give you a grace period,

664
00:20:57,825 --> 00:20:59,799
so if you pay the full statement

665
00:20:59,799 --> 00:21:01,279
balance by the due date,

666
00:21:01,279 --> 00:21:03,930
new purchases don't get charged interest at

667
00:21:03,930 --> 00:21:04,177
all.

668
00:21:04,527 --> 00:21:05,410
Rule two.

669
00:21:05,590 --> 00:21:06,716
Turn on autopay.

670
00:21:06,896 --> 00:21:08,274
At least the minimum,

671
00:21:08,274 --> 00:21:09,915
ideally the full balance.

672
00:21:10,095 --> 00:21:11,866
You'll never pay a late fee or

673
00:21:11,866 --> 00:21:13,578
trigger a penalty rate again.

674
00:21:13,758 --> 00:21:14,698
Rule three.

675
00:21:14,878 --> 00:21:15,900
Know your APR.

676
00:21:16,080 --> 00:21:16,962
Write it down.

677
00:21:17,142 --> 00:21:18,728
If you ever carry a balance,

678
00:21:18,728 --> 00:21:20,485
that's the number that matters,

679
00:21:20,485 --> 00:21:21,391
not the rewards.

680
00:21:21,741 --> 00:21:22,519
Rule four.

681
00:21:22,699 --> 00:21:24,269
If you carry a balance,

682
00:21:24,269 --> 00:21:26,931
attack it before anything else except a

683
00:21:26,931 --> 00:21:28,365
small emergency fund.

684
00:21:28,545 --> 00:21:30,430
Paying off a card at twenty two

685
00:21:30,430 --> 00:21:32,984
percent is a guaranteed twenty two percent

686
00:21:32,984 --> 00:21:33,410
return.

687
00:21:33,590 --> 00:21:36,004
Few investments can match that reliably.

688
00:21:36,184 --> 00:21:37,113
Rule five.

689
00:21:37,293 --> 00:21:39,842
Use balance transfers only with a written

690
00:21:39,842 --> 00:21:42,390
plan to finish before the promotion ends.

691
00:21:42,739 --> 00:21:44,678
Here's why rule four matters.

692
00:21:44,858 --> 00:21:47,310
A three thousand dollar balance at twenty

693
00:21:47,310 --> 00:21:48,028
two percent.

694
00:21:48,208 --> 00:21:50,771
Paying only around the minimum can take

695
00:21:50,771 --> 00:21:51,493
many years.

696
00:21:51,673 --> 00:21:53,713
Paying a fixed three hundred dollars a

697
00:21:53,713 --> 00:21:55,807
month clears it in about twelve months.

698
00:21:56,156 --> 00:21:57,074
Rule six.

699
00:21:57,254 --> 00:21:58,925
Never take a cash advance.

700
00:21:59,105 --> 00:22:01,217
Ask your issuer to lower or remove

701
00:22:01,217 --> 00:22:02,832
the cash limit if you can.

702
00:22:03,012 --> 00:22:03,895
Rule seven.

703
00:22:04,075 --> 00:22:07,319
Avoid deferred interest offers unless you'll pay

704
00:22:07,319 --> 00:22:08,333
them off early,

705
00:22:08,333 --> 00:22:10,767
with a fixed monthly amount set from

706
00:22:10,767 --> 00:22:11,308
day one.

707
00:22:11,657 --> 00:22:12,319
Rule eight.

708
00:22:12,499 --> 00:22:14,020
Match the card to your life.

709
00:22:14,200 --> 00:22:15,405
If you don't travel,

710
00:22:15,405 --> 00:22:17,212
you may not need a travel card

711
00:22:17,212 --> 00:22:18,356
with an annual fee.

712
00:22:18,536 --> 00:22:19,931
If you travel abroad,

713
00:22:19,931 --> 00:22:22,520
pick a card with no foreign transaction

714
00:22:22,520 --> 00:22:22,786
fee.

715
00:22:22,966 --> 00:22:25,088
A simple no fee card with flat

716
00:22:25,088 --> 00:22:27,423
cash back is the right answer for

717
00:22:27,423 --> 00:22:28,271
many people.

718
00:22:28,621 --> 00:22:29,399
Rule nine.

719
00:22:29,579 --> 00:22:31,596
Treat rewards as a discount,

720
00:22:31,596 --> 00:22:32,389
not a goal.

721
00:22:32,569 --> 00:22:34,368
Never spend to earn points.

722
00:22:34,548 --> 00:22:35,518
And once a year,

723
00:22:35,518 --> 00:22:37,397
add up what you earned and what

724
00:22:37,397 --> 00:22:39,216
you paid in fees and interest.

725
00:22:39,396 --> 00:22:41,470
If the second number is bigger,

726
00:22:41,470 --> 00:22:42,809
the card is winning.

727
00:22:42,989 --> 00:22:43,813
Rule ten.

728
00:22:43,993 --> 00:22:45,746
Read your statement every month.

729
00:22:45,926 --> 00:22:47,382
It takes five minutes,

730
00:22:47,382 --> 00:22:49,035
catches fraud and errors,

731
00:22:49,035 --> 00:22:51,152
and keeps you out of the ostrich

732
00:22:51,152 --> 00:22:51,615
effect.

733
00:22:51,965 --> 00:22:53,273
Do these ten things,

734
00:22:53,273 --> 00:22:56,018
and you become what the industry sometimes

735
00:22:56,018 --> 00:22:57,260
calls a transactor.

736
00:22:57,440 --> 00:23:00,017
Someone who uses the card for convenience

737
00:23:00,017 --> 00:23:01,085
and pays in full.

738
00:23:01,265 --> 00:23:03,564
You still get the protection and rewards.

739
00:23:03,744 --> 00:23:06,109
And the card company earns mostly from

740
00:23:06,109 --> 00:23:07,042
the swipe fees,

741
00:23:07,042 --> 00:23:08,411
not from you directly.

742
00:23:08,761 --> 00:23:11,078
Now, let's read a credit card statement

743
00:23:11,078 --> 00:23:12,860
the way the card company does.

744
00:23:13,040 --> 00:23:14,328
Five numbers matter.

745
00:23:14,678 --> 00:23:17,058
Number one, the statement balance.

746
00:23:17,238 --> 00:23:18,765
That's what you owe for the last

747
00:23:18,765 --> 00:23:19,433
billing cycle.

748
00:23:19,613 --> 00:23:21,444
Pay this full amount by the due

749
00:23:21,444 --> 00:23:23,394
date, and on most cards you won't

750
00:23:23,394 --> 00:23:24,930
pay interest on purchases.

751
00:23:25,110 --> 00:23:27,130
Number two, the current balance.

752
00:23:27,310 --> 00:23:29,707
That includes new purchases made after the

753
00:23:29,707 --> 00:23:30,677
statement closed.

754
00:23:30,857 --> 00:23:32,686
You don't have to pay those yet

755
00:23:32,686 --> 00:23:33,748
to avoid interest,

756
00:23:33,748 --> 00:23:34,456
but you can.

757
00:23:34,806 --> 00:23:37,036
Number three, the minimum payment.

758
00:23:37,216 --> 00:23:37,968
As we saw,

759
00:23:37,968 --> 00:23:39,398
this is the anchor.

760
00:23:39,578 --> 00:23:41,852
Paying only this keeps your account in

761
00:23:41,852 --> 00:23:44,605
good standing, but keeps the interest flowing.

762
00:23:44,785 --> 00:23:46,573
Number four, the due date.

763
00:23:46,753 --> 00:23:49,082
Miss it, and fees and possibly a

764
00:23:49,082 --> 00:23:50,538
penalty rate follow.

765
00:23:50,718 --> 00:23:52,599
Number five, the APR.

766
00:23:52,779 --> 00:23:54,395
Often there are several:

767
00:23:54,395 --> 00:23:55,606
one for purchases,

768
00:23:55,606 --> 00:23:57,356
one for balance transfers,

769
00:23:57,356 --> 00:23:58,837
one for cash advances,

770
00:23:58,837 --> 00:24:00,116
and a penalty rate.

771
00:24:00,466 --> 00:24:02,491
And then there's the box most people

772
00:24:02,491 --> 00:24:04,402
skip, the minimum payment warning.

773
00:24:04,582 --> 00:24:06,467
It shows how long it would take

774
00:24:06,467 --> 00:24:08,656
to pay off your balance with minimum

775
00:24:08,656 --> 00:24:10,724
payments only, and the total cost.

776
00:24:10,904 --> 00:24:12,858
It also shows what you'd need to

777
00:24:12,858 --> 00:24:14,629
pay each month to clear it in

778
00:24:14,629 --> 00:24:15,362
three years.

779
00:24:15,542 --> 00:24:17,340
That second number is often a much

780
00:24:17,340 --> 00:24:18,979
better target than the minimum.

781
00:24:19,159 --> 00:24:21,294
Try this: use the three year number

782
00:24:21,294 --> 00:24:22,514
as your new minimum.

783
00:24:22,863 --> 00:24:25,207
One more line worth checking every month

784
00:24:25,207 --> 00:24:26,613
is the interest charged.

785
00:24:26,793 --> 00:24:28,969
If that line shows anything other than

786
00:24:28,969 --> 00:24:31,260
zero, you're paying for the privilege of

787
00:24:31,260 --> 00:24:31,832
borrowing.

788
00:24:32,012 --> 00:24:34,685
Seeing that number in black and white,

789
00:24:34,685 --> 00:24:35,952
month after month,

790
00:24:35,952 --> 00:24:38,625
is often what finally pushes people to

791
00:24:38,625 --> 00:24:39,117
change.

792
00:24:39,297 --> 00:24:40,064
Circle it.

793
00:24:40,244 --> 00:24:41,544
Write it on a sticky note.

794
00:24:41,724 --> 00:24:44,464
Make it visible until it reaches zero.

795
00:24:44,814 --> 00:24:46,971
Let's make it real with two people

796
00:24:46,971 --> 00:24:49,255
holding the exact same rewards card,

797
00:24:49,255 --> 00:24:51,285
with two percent cash back and a

798
00:24:51,285 --> 00:24:52,744
twenty two percent APR.

799
00:24:52,924 --> 00:24:55,381
Alex spends about two thousand dollars a

800
00:24:55,381 --> 00:24:56,486
month on the card,

801
00:24:56,486 --> 00:24:58,820
and pays it off completely every month

802
00:24:58,820 --> 00:24:59,495
on autopay.

803
00:24:59,845 --> 00:25:00,674
Over a year,

804
00:25:00,674 --> 00:25:03,848
Alex earns roughly four hundred eighty dollars

805
00:25:03,848 --> 00:25:04,745
in cash back.

806
00:25:04,925 --> 00:25:06,573
He pays no interest,

807
00:25:06,573 --> 00:25:07,644
no late fees,

808
00:25:07,644 --> 00:25:09,128
and no annual fee.

809
00:25:09,308 --> 00:25:11,906
The card company still makes money from

810
00:25:11,906 --> 00:25:14,771
Alex, through swipe fees paid by merchants,

811
00:25:14,771 --> 00:25:16,436
but Alex comes out ahead.

812
00:25:16,616 --> 00:25:18,845
For him, the card is a tool.

813
00:25:19,195 --> 00:25:19,985
Now Jordan.

814
00:25:20,165 --> 00:25:22,499
Same card, same spending.

815
00:25:22,679 --> 00:25:24,717
But Jordan pays only part of each

816
00:25:24,717 --> 00:25:27,126
bill, and carries an average balance of

817
00:25:27,126 --> 00:25:28,855
around six thousand dollars.

818
00:25:29,035 --> 00:25:29,983
Twice a year,

819
00:25:29,983 --> 00:25:32,535
Jordan forgets a payment and gets a

820
00:25:32,535 --> 00:25:33,191
late fee.

821
00:25:33,541 --> 00:25:36,355
Jordan also earns around four hundred eighty

822
00:25:36,355 --> 00:25:37,698
dollars in cash back.

823
00:25:37,878 --> 00:25:40,110
But on a six thousand dollar average

824
00:25:40,110 --> 00:25:41,971
balance at twenty two percent,

