00:26:48,381 --> 00:26:50,835
Say you spend ten thousand dollars a

861
00:26:50,835 --> 00:26:52,607
year on travel and dining.

862
00:26:52,787 --> 00:26:54,327
At three points per dollar,

863
00:26:54,327 --> 00:26:56,038
that's thirty thousand points.

864
00:26:56,218 --> 00:26:57,970
Depending on how you redeem them,

865
00:26:57,970 --> 00:27:00,094
that might be worth around three hundred

866
00:27:00,094 --> 00:27:01,686
to four hundred fifty dollars.

867
00:27:01,866 --> 00:27:02,658
Add the credit,

868
00:27:02,658 --> 00:27:04,084
and you may come out ahead.

869
00:27:04,433 --> 00:27:06,339
But if you travel once a year,

870
00:27:06,339 --> 00:27:07,928
forget to use the credit,

871
00:27:07,928 --> 00:27:09,516
and rarely visit lounges,

872
00:27:09,516 --> 00:27:11,739
the same card can cost you hundreds

873
00:27:11,739 --> 00:27:13,899
of dollars a year for benefits you

874
00:27:13,899 --> 00:27:14,662
never touch.

875
00:27:14,842 --> 00:27:16,521
A simple no fee card with two

876
00:27:16,521 --> 00:27:18,836
percent cash back might leave you better

877
00:27:18,836 --> 00:27:19,068
off.

878
00:27:19,248 --> 00:27:21,338
The card company is betting you'll be

879
00:27:21,338 --> 00:27:22,355
the second person,

880
00:27:22,355 --> 00:27:23,485
and many people are.

881
00:27:23,835 --> 00:27:24,695
A simple rule.

882
00:27:24,875 --> 00:27:26,765
Only pay an annual fee if the

883
00:27:26,765 --> 00:27:29,178
benefits you actually used last year,

884
00:27:29,178 --> 00:27:31,330
in dollars, clearly beat the fee.

885
00:27:31,510 --> 00:27:32,506
And never, ever,

886
00:27:32,506 --> 00:27:34,187
pay an annual fee on a card

887
00:27:34,187 --> 00:27:35,805
where you carry a balance.

888
00:27:35,985 --> 00:27:36,860
At that point,

889
00:27:36,860 --> 00:27:38,110
you're paying twice.

890
00:27:38,290 --> 00:27:39,458
Once for the card,

891
00:27:39,458 --> 00:27:41,146
and once for the interest.

892
00:27:41,496 --> 00:27:43,763
Let me answer the questions people ask

893
00:27:43,763 --> 00:27:45,374
me most about credit cards.

894
00:27:45,724 --> 00:27:46,560
Question one.

895
00:27:46,740 --> 00:27:48,108
Should I just cut up all my

896
00:27:48,108 --> 00:27:48,412
cards?

897
00:27:48,592 --> 00:27:51,484
If cards consistently lead you into debt,

898
00:27:51,484 --> 00:27:53,671
using debit or cash for a while

899
00:27:53,671 --> 00:27:55,152
can be a smart reset.

900
00:27:55,332 --> 00:27:57,164
But if you can pay in full,

901
00:27:57,164 --> 00:27:59,064
cards offer real advantages,

902
00:27:59,064 --> 00:28:02,186
like stronger fraud protection than many debit

903
00:28:02,186 --> 00:28:04,562
cards, and building credit history.

904
00:28:04,742 --> 00:28:06,088
The card isn't the enemy.

905
00:28:06,268 --> 00:28:07,708
Carrying a balance is.

906
00:28:08,058 --> 00:28:09,034
Question two.

907
00:28:09,214 --> 00:28:11,141
Can I negotiate my interest rate?

908
00:28:11,321 --> 00:28:14,221
Often, yes, especially if you've paid on

909
00:28:14,221 --> 00:28:15,454
time for a while.

910
00:28:15,634 --> 00:28:18,143
Call, ask politely for a lower APR,

911
00:28:18,143 --> 00:28:21,011
and mention competing offers if you have

912
00:28:21,011 --> 00:28:21,369
them.

913
00:28:21,549 --> 00:28:22,928
It doesn't always work,

914
00:28:22,928 --> 00:28:24,847
but it costs a five minute call.

915
00:28:25,027 --> 00:28:27,421
Even a few percentage points can save

916
00:28:27,421 --> 00:28:28,974
real money on a balance.

917
00:28:29,324 --> 00:28:30,358
Question three.

918
00:28:30,538 --> 00:28:32,325
Does paying in full hurt my credit

919
00:28:32,325 --> 00:28:34,218
because the bank doesn't make money?

920
00:28:34,398 --> 00:28:34,642
No.

921
00:28:34,822 --> 00:28:36,786
Your credit score is based on things

922
00:28:36,786 --> 00:28:38,532
like paying on time and how much

923
00:28:38,532 --> 00:28:39,732
of your limit you use,

924
00:28:39,732 --> 00:28:41,532
not on how much interest you pay.

925
00:28:41,712 --> 00:28:43,489
Paying in full every month is one

926
00:28:43,489 --> 00:28:45,050
of the best things you can do

927
00:28:45,050 --> 00:28:45,857
for your score.

928
00:28:46,207 --> 00:28:47,032
Question four.

929
00:28:47,212 --> 00:28:48,791
When is the best time to pay?

930
00:28:48,971 --> 00:28:50,898
At minimum, by the due date.

931
00:28:51,078 --> 00:28:53,396
But your balance on the statement closing

932
00:28:53,396 --> 00:28:55,431
date is often what's reported to the

933
00:28:55,431 --> 00:28:56,279
credit bureaus.

934
00:28:56,459 --> 00:28:58,517
If you want a lower reported balance,

935
00:28:58,517 --> 00:29:00,964
paying before the statement closes can help.

936
00:29:01,144 --> 00:29:03,001
Either way, never pay late.

937
00:29:03,351 --> 00:29:04,396
Question five.

938
00:29:04,576 --> 00:29:06,399
Are store cards ever worth it?

939
00:29:06,579 --> 00:29:08,928
Sometimes, if you shop at that store

940
00:29:08,928 --> 00:29:10,364
often and pay in full.

941
00:29:10,544 --> 00:29:13,084
But store cards often have higher interest

942
00:29:13,084 --> 00:29:15,443
rates and deferred interest promotions.

943
00:29:15,623 --> 00:29:16,620
For most people,

944
00:29:16,620 --> 00:29:19,111
a general card with simple rewards works

945
00:29:19,111 --> 00:29:19,547
better.

946
00:29:19,897 --> 00:29:20,896
Question seven.

947
00:29:21,076 --> 00:29:23,508
Do card companies make money even if

948
00:29:23,508 --> 00:29:24,454
I pay in full?

949
00:29:24,634 --> 00:29:27,212
Yes, mainly through swipe fees paid by

950
00:29:27,212 --> 00:29:30,265
merchants, and sometimes through annual fees.

951
00:29:30,445 --> 00:29:32,325
That's why they're still happy to have

952
00:29:32,325 --> 00:29:32,523
you.

953
00:29:32,703 --> 00:29:34,305
But they make far less from you

954
00:29:34,305 --> 00:29:36,372
than from someone who carries a balance,

955
00:29:36,372 --> 00:29:38,438
and that's exactly the position you want

956
00:29:38,438 --> 00:29:38,903
to be in.

957
00:29:39,253 --> 00:29:40,623
And question six.

958
00:29:40,803 --> 00:29:42,781
What if I'm already deep in card

959
00:29:42,781 --> 00:29:43,091
debt?

960
00:29:43,271 --> 00:29:45,849
Start with a small emergency fund so

961
00:29:45,849 --> 00:29:47,926
new surprises don't add debt.

962
00:29:48,106 --> 00:29:49,906
Stop using the cards for now.

963
00:29:50,086 --> 00:29:51,918
List every balance and rate,

964
00:29:51,918 --> 00:29:53,685
and choose a payoff method.

965
00:29:53,865 --> 00:29:56,516
Call your issuers and ask about lower

966
00:29:56,516 --> 00:29:58,451
rates or hardship programs.

967
00:29:58,631 --> 00:30:00,524
And if you feel overwhelmed,

968
00:30:00,524 --> 00:30:03,635
look for a nonprofit credit counseling agency.

969
00:30:03,815 --> 00:30:06,299
Many offer free or low cost help.

970
00:30:06,649 --> 00:30:07,442
Before we go,

971
00:30:07,442 --> 00:30:09,638
five warning signs that your card is

972
00:30:09,638 --> 00:30:11,772
using you, instead of the other way

973
00:30:11,772 --> 00:30:12,199
around.

974
00:30:12,379 --> 00:30:12,600
One.

975
00:30:12,780 --> 00:30:14,045
You don't know your APR.

976
00:30:14,225 --> 00:30:14,480
Two.

977
00:30:14,660 --> 00:30:17,149
You've paid interest in three or more

978
00:30:17,149 --> 00:30:18,898
of the last twelve months.

979
00:30:19,078 --> 00:30:19,496
Three.

980
00:30:19,676 --> 00:30:21,932
You use one card to pay another,

981
00:30:21,932 --> 00:30:24,541
or take cash advances to cover bills.

982
00:30:24,890 --> 00:30:25,459
Four.

983
00:30:25,639 --> 00:30:28,373
You pick purchases to earn points or

984
00:30:28,373 --> 00:30:29,285
hit a bonus.

985
00:30:29,465 --> 00:30:30,382
And five.

986
00:30:30,562 --> 00:30:32,286
You feel a little relief when the

987
00:30:32,286 --> 00:30:33,592
minimum payment is small.

988
00:30:33,772 --> 00:30:35,646
If two or more of these sound

989
00:30:35,646 --> 00:30:38,230
familiar, it's time to run the playbook.

990
00:30:38,410 --> 00:30:40,338
Start with rule one this month:

991
00:30:40,338 --> 00:30:42,701
aim to pay the full statement balance,

992
00:30:42,701 --> 00:30:44,007
and if you can't yet,

993
00:30:44,007 --> 00:30:46,184
pick a fixed payment that clears it

994
00:30:46,184 --> 00:30:47,489
on a date you choose.

995
00:30:47,669 --> 00:30:49,713
Then put that date on your calendar

996
00:30:49,713 --> 00:30:51,524
and celebrate it when it comes.

997
00:30:51,873 --> 00:30:52,802
Quick recap.

998
00:30:52,982 --> 00:30:56,858
Credit card companies make money through interest,

999
00:30:56,858 --> 00:30:58,563
swipe fees, late fees,

1000
00:30:58,563 --> 00:31:01,277
annual fees, balance transfer fees,

1001
00:31:01,277 --> 00:31:05,230
cash advances, foreign transaction fees and penalty

1002
00:31:05,230 --> 00:31:05,695
rates.

1003
00:31:05,875 --> 00:31:08,268
More than one hundred billion dollars in

1004
00:31:08,268 --> 00:31:10,182
interest alone in a single year.

1005
00:31:10,532 --> 00:31:12,733
They keep it flowing with design:

1006
00:31:12,733 --> 00:31:14,533
the minimum payment anchor,

1007
00:31:14,533 --> 00:31:16,401
rewards that make you spend,

1008
00:31:16,401 --> 00:31:18,335
deferred interest promotions,

1009
00:31:18,335 --> 00:31:21,202
rising credit limits and painless payments.

1010
00:31:21,382 --> 00:31:23,067
And you flip the deal with ten

1011
00:31:23,067 --> 00:31:25,538
rules, starting with the most important one.

1012
00:31:25,718 --> 00:31:26,624
Pay in full,

1013
00:31:26,624 --> 00:31:28,435
every month, on autopay.

1014
00:31:28,785 --> 00:31:30,259
If you want the full plan,

1015
00:31:30,259 --> 00:31:32,412
from paying off cards to building real

1016
00:31:32,412 --> 00:31:33,998
wealth, it's in my playbook.

1017
00:31:34,178 --> 00:31:35,885
The link is in the description.

1018
00:31:36,065 --> 00:31:38,010
And if credit card debt is part

1019
00:31:38,010 --> 00:31:39,077
of a bigger loop,

1020
00:31:39,077 --> 00:31:41,148
watch my video on why most people

1021
00:31:41,148 --> 00:31:43,031
stay broke, and how to escape.

1022
00:31:43,211 --> 00:31:44,465
I'm Ryan Rover.

1023
